69
00:03:50,387 --> 00:03:55,233
[SPEAKER_05]: Yes, so in the affirmative, zero in the negative, consent agenda is approved.

70
00:03:55,914 --> 00:04:00,399
[SPEAKER_05]: We don't have any reports of subcommittees, but we do have a number of reports under our superintendent.

71
00:04:01,040 --> 00:04:06,427
[SPEAKER_05]: First up is recognition of Joan Bowen, director of student services, retirement.

72
00:04:06,807 --> 00:04:09,991
[SPEAKER_05]: I'm going to turn it over first to Dr. Suzanne Galluzzi, our superintendent.

73
00:04:11,725 --> 00:04:12,846
[SPEAKER_02]: Thank you very much, Mayor.

74
00:04:13,666 --> 00:04:18,189
[SPEAKER_02]: I do appreciate this time to acknowledge Joan Bowen.

75
00:04:18,229 --> 00:04:29,836
[SPEAKER_02]: This is a really bittersweet time for us, but I do want to make sure that we speak to her 33 years with us in Medford Public Schools.

76
00:04:30,476 --> 00:04:39,482
[SPEAKER_02]: And Joan, it's not just the incredible amount of time that you have given to this district that kind of like stands out for us.

77
00:04:40,222 --> 00:04:48,804
[SPEAKER_02]: but it is the profound amount of heart that you have poured into the community every single day.

78
00:04:48,824 --> 00:04:57,045
[SPEAKER_02]: You are also a resident as well as an employee, and I think that that has mattered tremendously.

79
00:04:57,065 --> 00:05:07,207
[SPEAKER_02]: You have worn a lot of hats over those three decades here at Medford Public Schools, from teacher to educational team leader to coordinator.

80
00:05:07,847 --> 00:05:25,358
[SPEAKER_02]: to the executive director and in every role you were a steady respected source of support for staff and a dedicated advocate to students and families and we knew that we were in capable

81
00:05:26,335 --> 00:05:27,395
[SPEAKER_02]: capable hands with you.

82
00:05:28,216 --> 00:05:37,579
[SPEAKER_02]: And that is a fact that even our Department of Education auditors and liaisons could not help but to notice and acknowledge.

83
00:05:38,519 --> 00:05:47,182
[SPEAKER_02]: And that is a level of trust that a lot of people had in you and something that cannot be underestimated.

84
00:05:48,102 --> 00:05:50,603
[SPEAKER_02]: And beyond all of your capable expertise,

85
00:05:51,580 --> 00:05:57,843
[SPEAKER_02]: I think, like I said before, what we will cherish the most is how deeply you cared for the Medford Public Schools community.

86
00:05:58,683 --> 00:06:06,587
[SPEAKER_02]: No matter how overwhelming your schedule got and how much demand was on your desk, um, you always made time.

87
00:06:07,367 --> 00:06:10,148
[SPEAKER_02]: You always made time for your colleagues to feel like a priority.

88
00:06:10,168 --> 00:06:11,849
[SPEAKER_02]: You always listened thoughtfully.

89
00:06:11,869 --> 00:06:18,472
[SPEAKER_02]: You helped to problem solve complex situations and you coached.

90
00:06:19,202 --> 00:06:21,543
[SPEAKER_02]: You coached well with care and skill.

91
00:06:22,363 --> 00:06:29,885
[SPEAKER_02]: And so you've positioned us well for your succession, but your impact of 33 years is deeply felt.

92
00:06:30,885 --> 00:06:41,927
[SPEAKER_02]: And we appreciate and thank you for your dedication, your heartfelt leadership, and your unwavering support and service to students and families and staff.

93
00:06:42,868 --> 00:06:47,989
[SPEAKER_02]: And so you will be missed, and we appreciate everything that you've given to us.

94
00:06:49,928 --> 00:06:52,435
[SPEAKER_02]: With a bittersweet heart, thank you, Joan.

95
00:06:54,420 --> 00:06:55,864
[SPEAKER_05]: And I'll second that.

96
00:06:56,546 --> 00:06:57,268
[SPEAKER_05]: You're also.

97
00:06:58,230 --> 00:07:01,251
[SPEAKER_05]: I'll second all that Dr. Galussi said and how humble you are.

98
00:07:01,311 --> 00:07:07,572
[SPEAKER_05]: You didn't want us to do anything big for you, so you said get this done virtually, which is crazy, Joan, but thank you.

99
00:07:07,592 --> 00:07:16,514
[SPEAKER_05]: 33 years is a long time and you have poured your heart into this job and you've made it better for hundreds and thousands of students.

100
00:07:16,734 --> 00:07:21,236
[SPEAKER_05]: So thank you for everything you've done for Bedford Public Schools, our students, our families.

101
00:07:21,736 --> 00:07:24,576
[SPEAKER_05]: Best of luck and have a wonderful retirement.

102
00:07:24,616 --> 00:07:25,397
[SPEAKER_05]: You so deserve it.

103
00:07:27,433 --> 00:07:32,437
[SPEAKER_05]: I think my schedule's hectic, but I know some of you over in the school department are much crazier.

104
00:07:32,537 --> 00:07:42,685
[SPEAKER_05]: So of course I have a virtual citation and then I'll give you a virtual hug after, but just to outline your dedicated service and congratulations on your retirement.

105
00:07:43,186 --> 00:07:44,327
[SPEAKER_05]: Thank you, I appreciate it.

106
00:07:44,347 --> 00:07:49,731
[SPEAKER_05]: Yep, and that was from the whole school committee.

107
00:07:49,811 --> 00:07:50,932
[SPEAKER_05]: Oh, Paul, member Rousseau.

108
00:07:52,146 --> 00:07:52,686
[SPEAKER_13]: Thank you.

109
00:07:54,568 --> 00:07:58,151
[SPEAKER_13]: You became the director right after I became a school committee member.

110
00:07:59,452 --> 00:08:04,397
[SPEAKER_13]: And so it was interesting because you were the first person I saw doing a transit.

111
00:08:04,417 --> 00:08:06,218
[SPEAKER_13]: Well, I mean, we had a new superintendent too.

112
00:08:08,595 --> 00:08:11,196
[SPEAKER_13]: It was your confidence and your calmness.

113
00:08:11,416 --> 00:08:15,777
[SPEAKER_13]: Calmness in particular has always been something that I've been in awe of.

114
00:08:16,877 --> 00:08:25,980
[SPEAKER_13]: I can't speak for, I certainly can't imagine what it's like to be any administrator, but if there was one job that I imagine is the hardest one, it is yours.

115
00:08:27,280 --> 00:08:32,602
[SPEAKER_13]: Because, well, you're not dealing with any situations that are easy, frankly.

116
00:08:33,883 --> 00:08:37,167
[SPEAKER_13]: And I've really appreciated that we've had, what do we have?

117
00:08:37,207 --> 00:08:43,493
[SPEAKER_13]: We had coordinated program reviews, then tiered focused monitoring, then targeted and focused monitoring.

118
00:08:43,934 --> 00:08:47,258
[SPEAKER_13]: And now apparently we have a new name, integrated monitoring review.

119
00:08:48,219 --> 00:08:52,964
[SPEAKER_13]: So DESI is keeping us on our toes to make sure that all the acronyms we could fathom

120
00:08:54,672 --> 00:08:56,693
[SPEAKER_13]: are through your department.

121
00:08:57,854 --> 00:09:02,858
[SPEAKER_13]: So I appreciate that those audits come back perfectly clean.

122
00:09:04,179 --> 00:09:16,387
[SPEAKER_13]: You know, it does as a school committee member to not have, well, to have the public or myself as a parent have to deal with findings that

123
00:09:18,704 --> 00:09:19,685
[SPEAKER_13]: require remediation.

124
00:09:19,705 --> 00:09:28,997
[SPEAKER_13]: I don't know how to, you know, sometimes, you know, those findings can really cause an uproar in the district and in the community.

125
00:09:29,037 --> 00:09:30,679
[SPEAKER_13]: So I'm glad we've not had that.

126
00:09:31,861 --> 00:09:32,922
[SPEAKER_13]: So I want to thank you for that.

127
00:09:33,022 --> 00:09:36,207
[SPEAKER_13]: But I also really want to thank you for your sense of humor.

128
00:09:36,367 --> 00:09:36,667
[SPEAKER_13]: It is

129
00:09:38,163 --> 00:09:48,638
[SPEAKER_13]: really a joy when we're in negotiations, when we're caucusing and you know you just you have an amazing sense of humor that can make what is often very stressful feel

130
00:09:49,723 --> 00:09:50,504
[SPEAKER_13]: less stressful.

131
00:09:50,664 --> 00:09:56,447
[SPEAKER_13]: And I'm guessing that's actually a part of how you are so calm in your current role.

132
00:09:56,968 --> 00:10:02,351
[SPEAKER_13]: And I am in a little bit of a denial that you're leaving us, but I'm glad you still live in Medford.

133
00:10:02,471 --> 00:10:04,713
[SPEAKER_13]: And I do hope that you will be around.

134
00:10:05,313 --> 00:10:09,276
[SPEAKER_13]: You're also a great hugger, but that's sort of a little out of bounds.

135
00:10:09,716 --> 00:10:16,361
[SPEAKER_13]: So thank you for all your service and for just for all of your service.

136
00:10:16,521 --> 00:10:16,801
[SPEAKER_13]: Thank you.

137
00:10:17,507 --> 00:10:18,007
[SPEAKER_05]: Thank you, members.

138
00:10:18,047 --> 00:10:20,109
[SPEAKER_05]: So I'm going to go to member Graham and then member Parks.

139
00:10:22,890 --> 00:10:23,391
[SPEAKER_03]: Thank you.

140
00:10:25,232 --> 00:10:26,773
[SPEAKER_03]: Joan, we do have a gift for you.

141
00:10:26,933 --> 00:10:34,017
[SPEAKER_03]: But because you haven't retired just yet, we're going to hold on to it because you're not gone just yet.

142
00:10:34,117 --> 00:10:43,703
[SPEAKER_03]: And we'll make sure we get that over to you in person instead of in a virtual hug kind of way before you leave, before your last day.

143
00:10:43,723 --> 00:10:46,865
[SPEAKER_03]: I just wanted to say thank you.

144
00:10:48,203 --> 00:10:54,246
[SPEAKER_03]: particularly for the work that you've had to do around the new Medford High School.

145
00:10:54,266 --> 00:11:09,955
[SPEAKER_03]: The special education planning is so interesting and intriguing because everywhere else along this process, there's sort of guidance, guidelines, formulas, et cetera.

146
00:11:10,075 --> 00:11:13,997
[SPEAKER_03]: And then you get to special education and it's just like blank slate.

147
00:11:16,082 --> 00:11:34,291
[SPEAKER_03]: And still, you have to balance, you know, sort of the difference between what you have and what you need and, you know, make the case for why students need what they need and balance all of the specialized programming that we provide at the high school.

148
00:11:34,391 --> 00:11:37,292
[SPEAKER_03]: And I know that's not been an easy feat and it's been.

149
00:11:38,108 --> 00:11:45,970
[SPEAKER_03]: many, many, many countless hours of meetings with the project team and answering lots of questions from everyone.

150
00:11:46,030 --> 00:11:53,312
[SPEAKER_03]: So I just really appreciate the work that you've done to set us up for the future.

151
00:11:55,013 --> 00:11:59,354
[SPEAKER_03]: And I hope that you'll come cut the ribbon with us.

152
00:12:02,775 --> 00:12:03,495
[SPEAKER_05]: Absolutely.

153
00:12:04,275 --> 00:12:04,715
[SPEAKER_05]: Thank you.

154
00:12:05,476 --> 00:12:05,776
[SPEAKER_05]: Thank you.

155
00:12:05,816 --> 00:12:07,116
[SPEAKER_05]: Member Parks and Member Olopade.

156
00:12:10,422 --> 00:12:11,283
[SPEAKER_04]: Thanks.

157
00:12:11,684 --> 00:12:28,459
[SPEAKER_04]: So Joan, I know I've known you probably the shortest in this group, but I just want to say that even though I'm new to this and I did come right at you from the start with the Special Ed Reserve Fund,

158
00:12:31,462 --> 00:12:37,205
[SPEAKER_04]: I just want to thank you so much for all of your support and everything that you do for Medford.

159
00:12:38,086 --> 00:12:40,988
[SPEAKER_04]: Just my brief interactions with you for that.

160
00:12:42,249 --> 00:12:59,520
[SPEAKER_04]: You were so generous with your time and your expertise, and just really always focused on getting everything right, explaining everything to me over and over, and making sure the numbers were right, the explanations were right, and just trying,

161
00:13:00,768 --> 00:13:13,873
[SPEAKER_04]: it was apparent to me that, you know, you wanted to get everything right for the kids and you've devoted basically, you know, a massive amount of your life doing it for Medford's kids.

162
00:13:14,033 --> 00:13:16,274
[SPEAKER_04]: And it really, I'm in awe of it.

163
00:13:16,494 --> 00:13:21,076
[SPEAKER_04]: So I just want to say Medford is stronger because of it and I appreciate it.

164
00:13:22,076 --> 00:13:25,197
[SPEAKER_04]: And I just wish you nothing but the best in your next adventure.

165
00:13:26,438 --> 00:13:26,858
[SPEAKER_14]: Thank you.

166
00:13:29,763 --> 00:13:30,643
[SPEAKER_05]: Member Olopade.

167
00:13:32,103 --> 00:13:32,544
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you.

168
00:13:32,604 --> 00:13:33,564
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you all hear me still?

169
00:13:34,804 --> 00:13:35,004
[SPEAKER_05]: Yes.

170
00:13:35,604 --> 00:13:36,724
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, I apologize.

171
00:13:37,505 --> 00:13:46,206
[SPEAKER_00]: I got a flat tire out in Harvard Mass, so I'm currently in an Uber driving back, and because of the dodgy connection, it seems like when I turn my camera on, my mic turns off.

172
00:13:46,246 --> 00:13:54,428
[SPEAKER_00]: So I've unfortunately will be a lifeless head for the moment, but that all to say that I want to echo I think what all of my colleagues have mentioned before.

173
00:13:54,448 --> 00:13:54,848
[SPEAKER_00]: I think

174
00:13:55,937 --> 00:14:07,325
[SPEAKER_00]: being a newer member and learning as much as I've learned these last few years, Joan, you've been a just endless reservoir of information and advice and suggestions and direction.

175
00:14:07,445 --> 00:14:17,472
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think that that is something that is just as invaluable as I can illustrate regarding my time in the school committee and I think the direction of the district.

176
00:14:17,892 --> 00:14:27,280
[SPEAKER_00]: I think we are stronger because of your service, and I believe that the impact you've given us over these last 33 years will be reverberating through the district for years to come.

177
00:14:27,841 --> 00:14:43,194
[SPEAKER_00]: And in some ways, I believe that your impact and your advocacy for this work and tireless education, I think, like many educators, is going to, in some ways, never be fully acknowledged or captured in full effect.

178
00:14:44,455 --> 00:14:50,117
[SPEAKER_00]: But I do hope that you hear me and my colleagues say, if nothing else, thank you, thank you, thank you.

179
00:14:50,598 --> 00:14:57,260
[SPEAKER_00]: It means more than you know, and I know the students and families, the caregivers, the guardians, the staff are thankful as well.

180
00:14:58,061 --> 00:15:08,245
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, I appreciate you and I hope that you'll be there to cut the ribbon with us, as member Graham mentioned, and you'll continue to be a resource, even though I want to take some time off.

181
00:15:09,005 --> 00:15:17,450
[SPEAKER_00]: I hope that you'll continue to support us, and we can be able to tap upon you as things come along, because I know that I'm going to miss you quite a bit.

182
00:15:17,971 --> 00:15:18,911
[SPEAKER_00]: So thank you again.

183
00:15:19,972 --> 00:15:20,812
[SPEAKER_05]: Thank you, Aaron.

184
00:15:21,953 --> 00:15:22,313
[SPEAKER_05]: Thank you.

185
00:15:22,754 --> 00:15:25,535
[SPEAKER_05]: Members, you have your hand up again, and then Member Reinfeldt.

186
00:15:26,216 --> 00:15:26,376
[SPEAKER_13]: Great.

187
00:15:26,396 --> 00:15:27,256
[SPEAKER_13]: I just have one quick thing.

188
00:15:28,237 --> 00:15:33,500
